NNAAMMEE
     mmiixxeerr — manipulate soundcard mixer controls

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmiixxeerr [--ff _d_e_v_i_c_e] [--dd _p_c_m_N | _N [--VV _v_o_s_s___d_e_v_i_c_e_:_m_o_d_e]] [--ooss]
           [_d_e_v[.._c_o_n_t_r_o_l[==_v_a_l_u_e]]] _._._.
     mmiixxeerr [--ooss] --aa
     mmiixxeerr --hh

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmiixxeerr utility is used to set and display soundcard mixer device con‐
     trols.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa                   Print the values for all mixer devices available in
                          the system (see _F_I_L_E_S).

     --dd _p_c_m_N | _N          Change the default audio card to _p_c_m_N, where N is
                          the unit number (e.g for pcm0, the unit number is
                          0).  See _E_X_A_M_P_L_E_S on how to list all available audio
                          devices in the system.

                          There is also the possibility of hot-swapping to the
                          new default device if virtual_oss(8) exists in the
                          system and is running, in which case the --VV option
                          needs to be specified as well.

                          Hot-swapping generally cannot happen with plain
                          sound(4), so the user has to restart the track in
                          order to get sound coming out of the new default de‐
                          vice.  This is because applications usually open a
                          device at the start of the track and do not check
                          for default device changes, in order to open the new
                          device mid-track.  virtual_oss(8), on the other
                          hand, can do hot-swapping, because it creates a vir‐
                          tual device for applications to open, and then does
                          all the necessary routing and conversions to the ap‐
                          propriate device(s).

                          Note that hot-swapping will work only for applica‐
                          tions that are using virtual_oss(8) devices, and not
                          plain sound(4) ones.

     --ff _d_e_v_i_c_e            Open _d_e_v_i_c_e as the mixer device (see _F_I_L_E_S).

     --hh                   Print a help message.

     --oo                   Print mixer values in a format suitable for use in‐
                          side scripts.  The mixer's header (name, audio card
                          name, ...) will not be printed.

     --ss                   Print only the recording source(s) of the mixer de‐
                          vice.

     --VV _v_o_s_s___d_e_v_i_c_e_:_m_o_d_e  Specify a virtual_oss(8) control device, as well as
                          a mode (see below), in order to hot-swap devices.
                          This option is meant to only be used in combination
                          with the --dd option.

                          The available modes are as follows:

                          MMooddee    AAccttiioonn
                          all     Playback and recording
                          play    Playback
                          rec     Recording

                          The _m_o_d_e part is needed, so that mmiixxeerr will not ac‐
                          cidentally hot-swap both the recording and playback
                          device in virtual_oss(8), if only one direction is
                          to be hot-swapped.

                          See _E_X_A_M_P_L_E_S on how to use this option.

     The list of mixer devices that may be modified are:

           vvooll, bbaassss, ttrreebbllee, ssyynntthh, ppccmm, ssppeeaakkeerr, lliinnee, mmiicc, ccdd, mmiixx, ppccmm22,
           rreecc, iiggaaiinn, ooggaaiinn, lliinnee11, lliinnee22, lliinnee33, ddiigg11, ddiigg22, ddiigg33, pphhiinn,
           pphhoouutt, vviiddeeoo, rraaddiioo, and mmoonniittoorr.

     Not all mixer devices are available.

     Without any arguments, mmiixxeerr displays all information for each one of the
     mixer's supported devices to _s_t_d_o_u_t.  If the _d_e_v argument is specified,
     mmiixxeerr displays only the values for _d_e_v.  More than one device may be
     specified.

     Commands use the following format:

           NNaammee                        AAccttiioonn
           _d_e_v                         Display all controls
           _d_e_v.._c_o_n_t_r_o_l                 Display only the specified control
           _d_e_v.._c_o_n_t_r_o_l==_v_a_l_u_e           Set control value

     The available controls are as follows (replace _d_e_v with one of the avail‐
     able devices):

           NNaammee                        VVaalluuee
           _d_e_v..vvoolluummee                  _v_o_l|[++|--]_l_v_o_l[%][::[++|--]_r_v_o_l[%]]
           _d_e_v..mmuuttee                    00|11|^^
           _d_e_v..mmuuttee                    ooffff|oonn|ttooggggllee
           _d_e_v..rreeccssrrcc                  ^^|++|--|==
           _d_e_v..rreeccssrrcc                  ttooggggllee|aadddd|rreemmoovvee|sseett

     The _d_e_v..vvoolluummee control modifies a device's volume.  The optional _l_v_o_l
     and/or _r_v_o_l values have to be specified.  The values should typically be
     decimal numbers between 0 and 1 with at most 2 digits after the decimal
     point.  A trailing percent sign indicates that the value should be
     treated as a percentage of 1.0, rather than an absolute value.  Thus, 70%
     means the same as 0.7.  If the left or right volume values are prefixed
     with ++ or --, the value following will be used as a relative adjustment,
     modifying the current settings by the amount specified.  Note that rela‐
     tive percentages are still relative to 1.0, not to the current value.  If
     the volume is currently 0.40 and an adjustment of +20% is specified, then
     thet final volume will be set to 0.60.

     Volume can also be set using the shorthand _d_e_v==vvaalluuee.  This syntax does
     not apply to other controls.

     The _d_e_v..mmuuttee control (un)mutes a device.  The following values are avail‐
     able:

           00 | ooffff     unmutes _d_e_v

           11 | oonn      mutes _d_e_v

           ^^ | ttooggggllee  toggles the mute of _d_e_v

     The _d_e_v..rreeccssrrcc control modifies the recording sources of a mixer.  mmiixxeerr
     marks devices which can be used as a recording source with rreecc.  Record‐
     ing sources are marked with ssrrcc.  To modify the recording source you can
     use one of the following modifiers on a rreecc device:

           ^^ | ttooggggllee  toggles _d_e_v of possible recording devices

           ++ | aadddd     adds _d_e_v to possible recording devices

           -- | rreemmoovvee  removes _d_e_v from possible recording devices

           == | sseett     makes _d_e_v the only recording device.

FFIILLEESS
     /dev/mixerN  The mixer device, where _N is the number of that device, for
                  example _/_d_e_v_/_m_i_x_e_r_0.  PCM cards and mixers have a 1:1 rela‐
                  tionship, which means that _/_d_e_v_/_m_i_x_e_r_0 is the mixer for
                  _/_d_e_v_/_p_c_m_0 and so on.  By default, mmiixxeerr prints both the au‐
                  dio card's number and the mixer associated with it in the
                  form of _p_c_m_N_:_m_i_x_e_r.  The _/_d_e_v_/_m_i_x_e_r file, although it does
                  not exist in the filesystem, points to the default mixer de‐
                  vice and is the file mmiixxeerr opens when the --ff _d_e_v_i_c_e option
                  has not been specified.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     List all available audio devices in the system:

           $ mixer -a | grep ^pcm

     Increase the vvooll device's volume by 5%:

           $ mixer vol=+5%

     Change the volume for the vvooll device of the _/_d_e_v_/_m_i_x_e_r_0 mixer device to
     0.65:

           $ mixer -f /dev/mixer0 vol.volume=0.65

     Increase the mmiicc device's left volume by 0.10 and decrease the right vol‐
     ume by 0.05:

           $ mixer mic.volume=+0.10:-0.05

     Toggle the mute for vvooll:

           $ mixer vol.mute=toggle

     Add mmiicc and remove lliinnee from the recording devices:

           $ mixer mic.recsrc=add line.recsrc=remove

     Dump _/_d_e_v_/_m_i_x_e_r_0 information to a file and retrieve back later:

           $ mixer -f /dev/mixer0 -o > info
           ...
           $ mixer -f /dev/mixer0 `cat info`

     Suppose virtual_oss(8) is running with _/_d_e_v_/_v_d_s_p_._c_t_l as its control de‐
     vice, and _p_c_m_0 as the playback device.  Change the default device to
     _p_c_m_1, and hot-swap to it for both recording and playback in
     virtual_oss(8):

           $ mixer -d pcm1 -V /dev/vdsp.ctl:all

